{
  "image_name": "329920fdba07bed98c354c10aa635a02.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how an organic donor–acceptor solar cell works, from device structure to the energetic pathway of charge generation. On the left, a layered device is shown with electrodes surrounding a mixed interfacial layer, where red and blue domains represent donor and acceptor materials interpenetrating each other. This morphology creates many interfaces where photoexcited states can separate.\n\nThe middle schematics explain the basic sequence. Light first creates an exciton in the donor, shown as a bound electron–hole pair between HOMO and LUMO levels. That exciton diffuses to a donor–acceptor boundary, where the energy offset between the two materials allows the electron to transfer to the acceptor while the hole remains in the donor. After this dissociation step, the separated charges travel through their respective phases and are collected at opposite electrodes.\n\nThe energy diagram on the right reframes the same process using excited-state levels. It shows optical excitation from the ground state to the singlet state, formation of charge-transfer states at the interface, and eventual generation of free charges. Several dashed arrows indicate competing pathways such as radiative or non-radiative relaxation and triplet-related routes. Overall, the figure emphasizes that efficient photocurrent depends on exciton diffusion to the interface and successful conversion of interfacial charge-transfer states into free carriers.",
